from svgkit import *
from router_figs import WIDE
F1,F2,F3,F4=WIDE
S=dict(slug='find-router-ip-address',date='2026-10-31',date_label='',plan='P14',
title="How to Find Your Router's IP Address on Any Device",headline="How to Find Your Router's IP Address on Windows, Mac, Linux, iPhone and Android",
desc="Find your router's IP address (the default gateway) on Windows, Mac, Linux, iPhone or Android, and what to do when the router page will not open.",
kw=["how to find router ip address","find router ip address","how do i find my router's ip address","default gateway","router ip address windows 11","192.168.1.1"],
about="Router IP address",section='Networking How-To',crumb="Find Your Router's IP Address",
h1='How to Find Your <span class="ndic-highlight">Router\'s IP Address</span>',tagline='Your router address is the default gateway. Pick your device and read it in seconds.',kicker='Routers &amp; Devices',cta='Pick My Device',
qa_title="Your router's IP address is your default gateway",
qa_body="Your router's IP address is the default gateway your device already uses. On Windows run ipconfig and read Default Gateway. On Mac use route -n get default, on Linux ip route, and on iPhone open Wi-Fi, tap the network's info icon and read Router. Common values are 192.168.0.1, 192.168.1.1 and 10.0.0.1.",
og_title='How to Find Your <span style="color:#38bdf8">Router\'s IP Address</span>',og_sub='Default gateway on Windows, Mac, Linux, iPhone and Android',og_chip='192 . 168 . 1 . <span style="color:#38bdf8">1</span>',
card_desc="Find your router's IP address (default gateway) on Windows, Mac, Linux, iPhone or Android, and fix a router page that will not open.",
picker_label='Choose your device',picker_note="Android menus differ by phone maker. If you do not see Gateway, open the network details and choose Advanced or View more.",
picker=[('Windows 10 / 11',['Press <strong>Windows + R</strong>, type <code>cmd</code> and press Enter.','Run <code>ipconfig</code>.','Under the adapter you are using (Wi-Fi or Ethernet), read <strong>Default Gateway</strong>. If two lines appear, use the IPv4 one.']),
('Mac',['Open <strong>Terminal</strong> and run <code>route -n get default</code>.','Read the <strong>gateway</strong> line.','Or open <strong>System Settings &gt; Wi-Fi &gt; Details &gt; TCP/IP</strong> and read <strong>Router</strong>.']),
('Linux',['Open a terminal and run <code>ip route | grep default</code>.','The address after <strong>via</strong> is the router.']),
('iPhone or iPad',['Open <strong>Settings &gt; Wi-Fi</strong>.','Tap the <strong>(i)</strong> next to your network.','Read the <strong>Router</strong> row.']),
('Android',['Open <strong>Settings &gt; Network &amp; internet &gt; Wi-Fi</strong>.','Tap the gear next to your network, then <strong>Advanced</strong> or <strong>View more</strong> if needed.','Read <strong>Gateway</strong> (some phones call it Router).']),
('No device handy',['Check the label on the router. Many print the admin address, such as 192.168.1.1, or a name such as routerlogin.net.','If there is no label, use any connected computer and one of the other options.'])],
panel=dict(eyebrow='Network Route panel',h3='Who owns your network?',p='A router has two addresses: a private one inside and a public one outside. The <a href="/intelligence/network-route">Network Route panel</a> shows who owns the public one.',href='/intelligence/network-route',btn='Open Network Route'),
sections=[
('find','Find It on Your Device',r'''<p>Pick your device above for the steps. This table shows the exact line to read, using 192.168.1.1 as the example. Your address may differ. To find another device on your network instead of the router, see the <a href="/guides/how-to-find-ip-address-of-any-device">guide to finding the IP address of any device</a>.</p>'''+table('Where to read your router address',['System','Run or open','The line to read'],[['Windows','<code class="ir-code">ipconfig</code> in Command Prompt','Default Gateway . . . : 192.168.1.1'],['Mac','<code class="ir-code">route -n get default</code> in Terminal','gateway: 192.168.1.1'],['Linux','<code class="ir-code">ip route | grep default</code>','default via 192.168.1.1 dev wlan0'],['iPhone and iPad','Settings, Wi-Fi, the info icon','Router 192.168.1.1'],['Android','Settings, Network &amp; internet, Wi-Fi, gear icon (Advanced or View more)','Gateway 192.168.1.1 (some phones: Router)']])),
('gateway','What the Router Address Really Is',r'''<p>When your device wants to reach anything outside your own network, it hands the traffic to one address: the default gateway. On a home network that is your router, so the router address and the default gateway are the same thing. Sending off-network traffic to a default gateway is standard host behaviour (RFC 1122). The router has a private address facing your devices and a public one facing the internet. The difference between them is covered in <a href="/learn/what-is-an-ip-address">What Is an IP Address?</a></p>'''+fig(F1,'The router sits on the boundary. Its LAN side is the gateway your devices use; its WAN side is the address the internet sees.')+table('Router address, gateway and public address',['Term','What it is','Example'],[['Router (LAN) address','The router\'s address inside your network','192.168.1.1'],['Default gateway','Where your device sends outside traffic. The same as the router LAN address','192.168.1.1'],['Public (WAN) address','The address the internet sees, from your ISP','203.0.113.7']])),
('ipconfig','Reading ipconfig Without Getting Lost',r'''<p>On Windows, <code class="ir-code">ipconfig</code> prints every adapter, including ones that are not connected. Find the adapter that has an IPv4 address and a Default Gateway. When IPv6 is on, the gateway appears twice: an address beginning fe80:: (an IPv6 link-local address, defined in RFC 4291) and the IPv4 address on the next line. The IPv4 line is the one you type into a browser.</p>'''+fig(F2,'Which lines of ipconfig to read, and which to skip.')),
('defaults','If You Cannot Check a Device',r'''<p>The router label is the most reliable source. If you cannot read it, the addresses routers most often use are 192.168.0.1, 192.168.1.1, 10.0.0.1 and 192.168.1.254. These are conventions, not rules, and an ISP box may use something else. Typing a wrong private address into a browser is harmless: it simply fails to load.</p>'''),
('open',"When the Router Page Will Not Open",r'''<p>If the address does not load, the fault is usually on your side of the connection. Check in this order.</p>'''+fig(F3,'The order of checks, from the most common cause to the least.')+table('Router page will not open',['Symptom','Likely cause','Fix'],[['Page times out','Phone is on mobile data or a VPN','Turn off mobile data and any VPN, and join the router\'s Wi-Fi or use a cable'],['Page does not load','Wrong address typed','Use the Default Gateway from your device, not a guess'],['Certificate or security warning','Router uses https with its own certificate','Try http:// first, or continue past the warning on your own router only'],['Works in one browser only','Cache or an extension is interfering','Use a private window or another browser'],['Nothing responds','Router needs a restart','Power it off for 30 seconds and try again, ideally over a cable']])),
('login','Login Details and Resetting a Router',r'''<p>Many routers print the admin name and password on a label. Some units print a unique password and others use a shared default, which is unsafe, so change it after you sign in. If you changed it and lost it, the reset button returns the router to factory settings.</p><div class="ir-callout"><p><strong>Before you reset:</strong> a reset erases your Wi-Fi name and password and any ISP login details. Have your ISP account details ready, and expect to set the router up again from scratch.</p></div><p>Once you are signed in, the router is also where you change DNS for every device on the network: see <a href="/learn/how-to-fix-slow-dns-lookup">how to fix slow DNS lookup</a>.</p>'''),
('dnat','Double NAT and Carrier-Grade NAT',r'''<p>If two routers each translate addresses, you have double NAT. It usually breaks port forwarding and can make online games report a strict connection. To check, sign in to your router and read the address on its Internet or WAN page. A private address there (192.168.x.x, 10.x.x.x or 172.16.x.x to 172.31.x.x, the ranges in RFC 1918) usually means another device is translating in front of yours. An address from 100.64.0.0 to 100.127.255.255 means your ISP uses carrier-grade NAT (RFC 6598), and only the ISP can change that.</p>'''+fig(F4,'In double NAT the second router\'s WAN address is private.')+r'''<p>The usual fix is to put the ISP box in bridge mode, or to set your second router to access-point mode.</p>'''),
('public','The Router Address Is Not the Address Websites See',r'''<p>The router address you found here is the private side. The address websites see is your public address, shown in the <a href="/intelligence/network-identity">Network Identity panel</a>.</p><p>Many ISP boxes combine the modem and the router in one device, and the gateway for your devices is the router part. If you are looking for a printer rather than the router, use the <a href="/learn/find-printer-ip-address">printer IP guide</a>.</p>'''),
('ipv6','IPv6 Gateways Look Different',r'''<p>On IPv6 the default gateway is often a link-local address beginning fe80::. It works, but it is not what you type into a browser. Use the IPv4 gateway shown in the same output to open the router page.</p>''')],
logic="Define the term first (router address equals default gateway), show the exact line to read on each device, then cover what goes wrong: a page that will not open, a lost login, double NAT, and the private versus public mix-up.",
method="Commands and menu names follow the default behaviour of current Windows, macOS, Linux, iOS and Android. Phone menus vary by maker and version, so the page names the label to look for. The ipconfig behaviour is described in Microsoft's reference. Default addresses are listed as conventions only. Steps were not tested on every router or phone model.",
cites=[("Microsoft Learn: ipconfig",'https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/ipconfig'),("RFC 1918: Address Allocation for Private Internets",'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918'),("RFC 6598: IANA-Reserved IPv4 Prefix for Shared Address Space",'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6598'),("RFC 4291: IP Version 6 Addressing Architecture",'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4291'),("RFC 1122: Requirements for Internet Hosts (routing to a default gateway)",'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122')],
faq_sub='Thirteen questions on router addresses, gateways, logging in and double NAT.',
faq=[("Is the router IP address the same as the default gateway?","On a home network, yes. The default gateway is the router's address on your local network."),
("Why is my router address 192.168.1.1 or 192.168.0.1?","These are common defaults from the private ranges in RFC 1918. They are conventions, so your router may use another address."),
("Is the router address my public IP address?","No. The router address is private and works only inside your network. Your public address is assigned by your ISP and shown in the Network Identity panel."),
("The address does not open in my browser. What now?","Work through the table above: be on the router's own network with mobile data and VPN off, use the exact Default Gateway, try http:// in a private window, then restart the router."),
("I cannot find Gateway on my Android phone.","Open the network details and look under Advanced or View more. Some phones label it Router."),
("Why does ipconfig show two Default Gateway lines?","One is an IPv6 link-local address beginning fe80:: and the other is the IPv4 address. Use the IPv4 line to open the router page."),
("Does the router address ever change?","Rarely. It is set on the router. The addresses of your devices change more often."),
("What is the difference between a modem and a router?","A modem connects to your ISP, and a router shares that connection with your devices. Many ISP boxes combine both."),
("Can I find the router address without being connected?","No. A device needs to be connected to the network. If you are locked out, read the label or reset the router."),
("Is it safe to share my router's private address?","It is low risk because it only works inside your network, but the admin login should still be changed from the default."),
("What is double NAT and how do I know I have it?","It means two routers both translate addresses. If your router's WAN address is private (192.168.x.x or 10.x.x.x) rather than public, you have it. Put the first device in bridge mode or the second in access-point mode."),
("My router's WAN address starts with 100.64 to 100.127. What does that mean?","Your ISP uses carrier-grade NAT, so many customers share one public address. Port forwarding will not work, and only your ISP can change it."),
("Should I factory reset my router?","Only as a last resort for a lost password. A reset erases Wi-Fi settings and ISP login details, so keep your ISP account information ready first.")])
